## 2. Licences requiring attention

Nothing in this section is a legal opinion. It is an inventory with the
reasoning made explicit, so that a decision can be taken deliberately.

### 2.1 Network copyleft — the item with real consequences

| Package | Licence | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| `shepherd.js` | **AGPL-3.0** | Used for the in-product tour. GPLv3 §13 permits combining GPLv3 code with AGPLv3 code, but the AGPL obligations continue to apply to that part — including **offering the corresponding source to users who interact with it over a network**. Dynadoc has a web deployment, so this is not theoretical. Options: keep it and satisfy the AGPL offer for the web build, replace it (several MIT tour libraries exist), or buy a commercial licence from the author. |
| `citeproc` | **CPAL-1.0 OR AGPL-1.0** | Citation formatting. Both branches are copyleft with a network/attribution clause; neither is a plain permissive licence. CPAL-1.0 additionally requires a visible attribution notice in the user interface. To be reviewed on the same footing as `shepherd.js`. |

### 2.2 Strong copyleft, compatible but constraining

| Package | Licence | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| `hyperformula` | **GPL-3.0-only** | Spreadsheet formula engine. Same licence as Dynadoc, so distribution is fine today. Two consequences worth stating: `-only` means this dependency cannot be taken under "GPLv3 or later" terms, which constrains any future licence upgrade; and because its author offers a separate commercial licence, **any non-GPL or proprietary edition of Dynadoc would need that commercial licence or a different engine.** For an open-core model this is the dependency to watch. |

### 2.3 Non-OSI licence inside a GPLv3 distribution

| Package | Licence | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| `gsap` | **Custom (GreenSock standard licence)** | Animation library. The GreenSock licence is not an OSI-approved licence and is not GPL-compatible: it imposes conditions that the GPL does not permit to be added to a combined work. Distributing it inside a GPLv3 application is a **licence-compatibility conflict**, independently of whether the current terms allow free use. Options: replace it (the Web Animations API, `motion`, or `animejs` cover most uses), isolate it so it is not part of the combined work, or obtain a written exception from the author. |
| `buffers` | Custom, by URL only | Transitive, tiny, unmaintained. The "licence" is a link to a repository. Worth removing from the tree rather than reasoning about. |

### 2.4 No licence declared

| Package | Licence | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| `hyphenation.de` | **none found** | Hyphenation patterns. With no licence, the default is "all rights reserved": there is no grant to redistribute. The underlying TeX hyphenation patterns are generally permissively licensed, so the fix is probably to identify the upstream source and record it — but it has to be done, not assumed. |
| `hyphenation.en-us` | **none found** | Same. |

### 2.5 Attribution required — satisfied by this file

